WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.575
The same logic applies to administrators,
and this is where most breaches go.

2
00:00:04.575 --> 00:00:07.757
A standing administrator
is always on, at any hour,

3
00:00:07.757 --> 00:00:11.637
so any stolen credential that
reaches the role gets all of it.

4
00:00:11.637 --> 00:00:16.924
Stronger sign-in helps, but it doesn't
narrow what the role can do, where, or when.

5
00:00:16.924 --> 00:00:19.387
An eligible administrator is different.

6
00:00:19.387 --> 00:00:24.585
The role is switched on only when needed,
for a set time, approved by someone else,

7
00:00:24.585 --> 00:00:28.424
after a fresh strong sign-in,
and every elevation is logged.

8
00:00:28.424 --> 00:00:32.743
NSA and CISA rank poor separation
of admin privilege among the most

9
00:00:32.743 --> 00:00:37.288
common misconfigurations they find,
and NIST requires least privilege.

10
00:00:37.288 --> 00:00:42.269
There's one standing exception: two
break-glass accounts stay permanently active,

11
00:00:42.269 --> 00:00:45.638
so the tenant can be recovered
if everything else fails.

12
00:00:45.638 --> 00:00:49.132
They're safe only when
governed: phishing-resistant,

13
00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:51.813
monitored, and tested every ninety days.

14
00:00:51.813 --> 00:00:54.290
Next, what the policy engine looks at.
